"<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\"><title>HomeTruth discussion guide</title><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#faf7ef;color:#302c25;font:16px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif}header{padding:30px 36px;background:#5b4325;color:#fff}header h1{margin:0;font-size:29px}header p{margin:8px 0 0;color:#f1e2c9;max-width:850px}.layout{display:grid;grid-template-columns:235px minmax(0,850px);gap:28px;padding:28px;justify-content:center}nav{position:sticky;top:20px;align-self:start}nav a{display:block;color:#654c2a;padding:8px 10px;text-decoration:none;border-radius:6px}nav a:hover{background:#eee4d2}.step{background:white;border:1px solid #e1d6c4;border-radius:12px;padding:25px;margin-bottom:24px;scroll-margin-top:20px}.step h2{font-size:22px;margin:0 0 8px}.time{font-size:13px;font-weight:500;color:#7b6749}.label{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.3px;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#80613b;margin-top:20px}p{margin:6px 0 12px}blockquote{margin:8px 0;padding-left:18px;border-left:3px solid #bd9455;font-size:17px}.buttons{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-top:14px}button{font:600 13px system-ui;border:1px solid #c6b394;background:#fffaf0;color:#654c2a;padding:10px 13px;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer}button:hover{background:#f3e4c9}.note{background:#f7f3eb;border-radius:7px;padding:12px 15px;font-size:14px}.question{color:#754819;font-weight:600}.intro{margin:0 0 22px;padding:18px;background:#eee5d5;border-radius:8px;font-size:14px}footer{font-size:13px;color:#78684e;margin:10px 0 35px}@media(max-width:800px){.layout{grid-template-columns:1fr;padding:16px}nav{position:static;display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:5px}nav a{font-size:13px}.step{padding:20px}}@media print{nav,.buttons{display:none}.layout{display:block}.step{break-inside:avoid}}</style></head><body><header><h1>Before the demo / Discussion guide</h1><p>A 10–12 minute conversation: start with homeowner value, explain the evidence model, then show the database behind it.</p></header><div class=\"layout\"><nav aria-label=\"Guide sections\"><a href=\"#step-1\">1. The homeowner’s problem</a><a href=\"#step-2\">2. Show the whole picture</a><a href=\"#step-3\">3. The home and who can access it</a><a href=\"#step-4\">4. Evidence and trust</a><a href=\"#step-5\">5. From knowledge to useful action</a><a href=\"#step-6\">6. How concepts become real data</a><a href=\"#step-7\">7. Set expectations, then begin the demo</a></nav><main><div class=\"intro\"><strong>Presenter’s controls</strong><br>Use the buttons below to open the exact view being discussed. “Back to discussion guide” returns here without losing your place. In a diagram, hover to trace connections; click to pull them out; Reset restores the overview.</div><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-1\"><h2>1. The homeowner’s problem</h2><span class=\"time\">1 minute</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Start with the benefit, before explaining the model.</p><div class=\"buttons\"></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>Information about a home is scattered across documents, emails, people and conversations. It’s difficult to know what’s reliable, what’s missing and what needs attention. HomeTruth brings that information together around the home, preserves the evidence behind it, and helps the homeowner decide what to do next.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Ask</div><p class=\"question\">When you need to answer a question about your home today, where do you look—and how do you know you’ve found the right answer?</p><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">Keep this grounded in the person’s experience. Don’t start with database terminology.</p></section><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-2\"><h2>2. Show the whole picture</h2><span class=\"time\">1 minute</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Open Ontology → Overview — show all. Hover over Property to trace its connections without moving cards.</p><div class=\"buttons\"><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"\" data-mode=\"all\">Ontology overview ↗</button></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>This map shows the concepts HomeTruth connects. At its centre are a property, the people connected to it, and the evidence describing it. Ontology simply means a shared vocabulary for the things we care about and how they relate.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">Green cards map to existing tables. Amber dashed cards represent planned or conceptual extensions. Hover traces connections in place; click pulls out connected cards. Avoid reading every card.</p></section><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-3\"><h2>3. The home and who can access it</h2><span class=\"time\">2 minutes</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Select Property, then Property relationship. Use the full overview or switch to Focus for fewer distractions.</p><div class=\"buttons\"><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"property\" data-mode=\"all\">Show Property ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"role\" data-mode=\"focus\">Show Property relationship ↗</button></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>The home is a durable record. People connect to it through explicit relationships—such as owner or tenant—rather than the home being just an address on someone’s account. The relationship represents their role and permissions for a particular home. Knowing who someone is and knowing which home they can access are separate questions.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">Benefit: a foundation for several people connected to one home and one person connected to several homes. A global user role alone does not establish property access.</p></section><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-4\"><h2>4. Evidence and trust</h2><span class=\"time\">3 minutes</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Select Property fact in Focus. Follow Evidence source → Property document. Then show Conversation.</p><div class=\"buttons\"><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"fact\" data-mode=\"focus\">Property fact ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"evidence\" data-mode=\"focus\">Evidence source ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"link\" data-mode=\"focus\">Property document ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"chat\" data-mode=\"focus\">Conversation ↗</button></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>A property fact is a structured claim—for example, an EPC rating or a recorded service date. We need to know where it came from, when it applies and how it was verified. An uploaded invoice is a document. We link it to the home; a particular passage can support a claim about work carried out. But an invoice does not necessarily prove payment. A missing service receipt means the date is unknown—it does not automatically mean the service is overdue. A saved conversational answer is useful, but it does not automatically become a verified property fact.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Ask</div><p class=\"question\">Is the distinction between a document, a claim and its supporting evidence clear?</p><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">If asked: the current property fact record can reference one evidence source. A persisted relationship supporting several sources per fact is a future extension. Confidence is not the same as verification.</p></section><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-5\"><h2>5. From knowledge to useful action</h2><span class=\"time\">1–2 minutes</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Show Task / reminder, then Physical home item and Internal agent run. Only show Delegated agent grant if personal agents come up.</p><div class=\"buttons\"><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"task\" data-mode=\"focus\">Task / reminder ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"item\" data-mode=\"focus\">Physical home item ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"run\" data-mode=\"focus\">Internal agent run ↗</button><button data-view=\"ontology\" data-node=\"grant\" data-mode=\"focus\">Optional: delegated grant ↗</button></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>The practical value is helping someone act: understand an open task, identify an appliance, find a manual or retrieve an answer later. For an appliance lookup, we distinguish the physical item, the operation performed and the evidence proposed for review. Each has a different purpose and lifecycle.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">External personal-agent access remains roadmap work. It needs a separate revocable grant. An active grant must not override lost property or document access. Partner programme consent is not an external-agent grant.</p></section><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-6\"><h2>6. How concepts become real data</h2><span class=\"time\">2 minutes</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Switch to Database → Overview. Select properties → property_people → property_facts. Use the panel to inspect columns and indexes.</p><div class=\"buttons\"><button data-view=\"database\" data-node=\"\" data-mode=\"all\">Database overview ↗</button><button data-view=\"database\" data-node=\"properties\" data-mode=\"all\">properties ↗</button><button data-view=\"database\" data-node=\"property_people\" data-mode=\"focus\">property_people ↗</button><button data-view=\"database\" data-node=\"property_facts\" data-mode=\"focus\">property_facts ↗</button></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>The purple view shows the physical database behind these concepts: this snapshot contains 45 actual local tables. Lines show database-enforced foreign keys. The ontology explains meaning; this view explains storage and structural relationships. Some application relationships are not enforced by foreign keys, so they will not appear as lines. The properties table holds the home record; property_people connects users to homes; property_facts stores structured claims and an evidence reference.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">Keep this short for nontechnical audiences. If naming comes up: a plural table holds many records; each row is one instance. Indexes support efficient retrieval. A foreign key helps enforce structural integrity; it does not establish the truth of a claim.</p></section><section class=\"step\" id=\"step-7\"><h2>7. Set expectations, then begin the demo</h2><span class=\"time\">30 seconds</span><div class=\"label\">Show / click</div><p>Return to the discussion guide, summarise the three points below, then move to the local app.</p><div class=\"buttons\"></div><div class=\"label\">Say</div><blockquote>This currently runs locally. We don’t yet have hosted staging or production. The diagrams distinguish implemented storage from planned concepts; they are not a claim that every workflow is finished. In the demo, we’ll follow one homeowner question: open relevant information, ask a question, inspect what supports the answer, and see what remains unknown. Let’s see whether this structure makes a homeowner’s question easier to answer.</blockquote><div class=\"label\">Presenter note</div><p class=\"note\">Three points to carry into the demo: the home is the organising record; evidence determines what we can confidently say; access is specific to the person and property.</p></section><footer>Based on the local database and ontology snapshot captured 19 November 2026. Table mappings establish storage, not proof of every workflow’s readiness.</footer></main></div><script>document.querySelectorAll(\"button[data-view]\").forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>parent.postMessage({type:'ontology-guide-jump',view:b.dataset.view,node:b.dataset.node,mode:b.dataset.mode}, '*'));</script></body></html>"
